---
/**
 * TagFilters — the whole tag vocabulary, at the top of a library list.
 *
 * **The page used to have nothing at the top you could press.** `KindTabs`
 * partitions the section four ways and that was the only filter a reader could
 * see without first finding a row about the right subject and pressing the chip
 * on it. Aayush's review named it: the tags were reachable only from inside the
 * list, so finding everything about `agents` meant already having found one.
 *
 * design.md §3 said the absence was deliberate — "a /sites entry hides its
 * collections behind a details page, so the index has to offer them somewhere,
 * while a library row wears its tags in the list itself" — and that paragraph
 * is now written up as the reversal it is. Wearing a tag on a row tells you
 * what *that* row is about. It does not tell you the vocabulary, and it cannot
 * offer a subject that is not on the screen.
 *
 * ## Why this is chips and `KindTabs` is tabs
 *
 * A kind is a partition: every entry has exactly one, the four tabs add up to
 * the list, and a tab row is the idiom for "where you are". Tags overlap and do
 * not add up to anything — a link can carry three or none — so a row of them is
 * a set of doors rather than a set of positions, which is what a chip already
 * is everywhere else on this page. Reusing `.tag` is the point: the chip a
 * reader presses at the top and the chip they press on a row are visibly one
 * control, and pressing either lands on the same page.
 *
 * Every tag, on every one of these pages, not just the ones present in what is
 * being shown. Same call `KindTabs` makes and for the same reason: a filter row
 * that changes between the page you filtered from and the page you landed on is
 * two controls that happen to resemble each other. `lib/library.ts ›
 * libraryTags` is the vocabulary and it is alphabetical, so nothing here
 * reorders under a reader between visits.
 *
 * The selected state is `aria-current="page"`, which is the honest way for a
 * link to say the reader is already there, and `styles/chip.css` closes the
 * dotted border to say it in ink. There is no `All` chip: `All` is what the tab
 * row already offers one line above, and on a tag page the crumb is the way
 * back. A second one here would be a third control saying the same thing.
 */
import TagGlyph from "./TagGlyph.astro";
import type { LibraryTagGroup } from "../lib/library";
import { hueSlot, tagLabel } from "../lib/tags";

interface Props {
  /** Every tag with a page, alphabetically: `lib/library.ts › libraryTags`. */
  groups: LibraryTagGroup[];
  /** The tag this page is showing, or null anywhere else. */
  current: string | null;
}

const { groups, current } = Astro.props;
---

{/*
  Links with `aria-current`, never `role="tab"` — the call `KindTabs.astro`
  makes at length. These navigate to real pages, and a tablist role promises
  arrow-key movement inside the row that a static site does not implement.
*/}
<nav class="filters" aria-label="Filter the library by tag">
  <ul class="filters__row">
    {
      groups.map((group) => (
        <li>
          <a
            class="tag"
            data-hue={hueSlot(group.slug)}
            href={`/library/tag/${group.slug}`}
            aria-current={current === group.slug ? "page" : undefined}
          >
            <TagGlyph slug={group.slug} />
            {tagLabel(group.slug)}
          </a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</nav>

<style>
  /* The gap to the tab row above, owned here rather than by the pages that
     render this. `--gap-masthead` is the lesson: one number written out in
     three scoped blocks is a number nobody can retune. It is tighter than the
     1.25rem under it because the tabs and these chips are both filters and the
     list below is the thing being filtered.

     A custom property with a default, because on a tag page there is no tab row
     to sit under — a tag cuts across all three kinds — and the row is the first
     thing after the masthead there. That page sets `--filters-gap: 0` so
     `--gap-masthead` stays exactly the space under a masthead rather than
     becoming that plus fourteen pixels. A property inherits through Astro's
     scoping; a `:global` override would be the other way to do it and it is
     the one design.md §5 keeps telling us not to reach for. */
  .filters {
    margin-top: var(--filters-gap, 0.9rem);
  }

  /* Wraps rather than scrolls, the call `.tabs__row` and `.foot__meta` both
     make: a flex row that cannot wrap pushes its last item off the side of a
     375px column instead of dropping it to a second line. Twelve chips are
     four lines at that width.

     The 1.25rem row gap is `TagChips.astro`'s arithmetic and not a rhythm
     chosen here: each chip claims a 40px hit area through an `::after` that
     reaches 0.62rem above and below it, so two lines closer than 1.25rem would
     have overlapping targets and the upper chip would eat presses meant for the
     lower one. `lib/tags.test.mjs` reads the overreach out of `chip.css` and
     fails if this number stops covering it.

     **The column gap is 0.5rem where it was 0.4rem**, which is Aayush's review
     of this row: the leading looked weird, and a 6.4px column gap under a 20px
     row gap is a field packed tighter across than a chip's own side padding and
     three times looser down. It cannot be made even — the row gap is a hit area
     and the column gap is not — but it can stop being 3:1, and the other half of
     that is the line below. */
  .filters__row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1.25rem 0.5rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* **The other half of the leading, and it is all subtraction.** An `li` is a
     block box, so an `inline-flex` chip inside one sits in a *line box* — and a
     line box is as tall as the inherited strut, which is 16px of Geist at 1.6.
     Measured on the shipped row: a 20.27px chip inside a 25.59px item, so every
     wrapped line carried 5.32px of leading nobody chose and the pitch came out
     at 45.59px for a chip 20.27px tall. Making the item a flex container
     blockifies the chip and the strut goes with the line box.
     `TagChips.astro › .tags li` does the same thing for the same reason.

     What is left between two lines is the row gap and nothing else, which is
     the point: that number is a hit area, it is *why* the row is as open as it
     is, and it should not have had a second, invisible number added on top of
     it. Pitch 45.59px to 40.27px, and the targets now touch with a sixth of a
     pixel to spare instead of hiding five. */
  .filters__row li {
    display: flex;
  }
</style>
